  "body": "### What are you trying to do\n\nI run a 3-axis mill on LinuxCNC 2.9 (Remora controller, `M6` remapped to an automatic tool changer) and would like to post from CamCraft straight to it. There is no plain LinuxCNC post today. The Tormach PathPilot post is the closest, but it carries Tormach assumptions, so its output has to be checked and hand-edited before it is safe on a stock LinuxCNC machine. #435 / #495 is one example: the retract assumed machine Z0 is the top of travel.\n\n### How you imagine it working\n\nA generic LinuxCNC mill post (e.g. `linuxcnc.cps` plus a `linuxcnc-3axis.mch` machine), written to the RS274NGC dialect as LinuxCNC documents it, with no PathPilot-specific codes. LinuxCNC machines differ a lot from one another, so the machine-specific parts would be options rather than fixed:\n\n- **Retract and tool-change position:** `G53 G0 Z<value>` with a user value (machine Z0 is not always the top of travel), `G28`/`G30`, or none.\n- **Tool change:** `T<n> M6` then `G43 H<n>`, with an option to leave out `G43` when the `M6` remap applies the tool length itself.\n- **Path blending:** `G64 P<tolerance>` with a user value, or `G61` exact path.\n- **Program start/end:** a safe block (`G17 G90 G40 G49 G80`, units, work offset); end with `M5 M9` and `M2` or `M30`; optional `%` delimiters.\n- **Drilling and tapping:** `G81`/`G82`/`G83`/`G73`. LinuxCNC's rigid tap is `G33.1`, which needs a spindle encoder, so an option to refuse or expand taps on a machine without one.\n- **Arcs:** `G2`/`G3` with incremental IJK (LinuxCNC's default `G91.1`), helices allowed.\n- Comments in parentheses, no line numbers by default; spindle `M3`/`M4 S`, coolant `M7`/`M8`/`M9`.\n\nHappy to run posted programs through a LinuxCNC 2.9 sim and air-cut them on my machine, and report back.\n",
